steveyo <steveyo@cannedmeat.nycap.rr.com> deserves a cookie for saying:
> I've noticed a relationship of monsters and treasure type, i.e. giants carry
> gems and nymphs carry mirrors, but what about random treasure dropped by
> slain monsters?

relobj() in steal.c deals with dropping the monster's inventory.  

The inventory is initialized in m_initinv(), m_initthrow(), and
m_initweap() in makemon.c.  

That will lead you to rnd_offensive_item(), rnd_defensive_item(), and
rnd_misc_item() in muse.c.  Those functions are fairly explicit on item
generation.  

Also, there's a chance to make a random item on a monster's death in
xkilled() in mon.c around line 1805 or so.  

> Where in the code are the treasure probabilities?  

For normal generation?  Probabilities are stored in the objects[] array,
which is initialized in objects.c, IIRC.  For monster drops, see the
above functions.

> Do they depend on dungeon leve1?

Well, each item type can have different probabilities depending on
whether you're in gehennom, whether it's in a chest, etc...  But
generally, they don't vary based strictly on dlvl, no.  Monster drops
can vary by monster difficulty since that's how the rnd_*() functions
work.

> Also, where in the code is a new level generated?

Start in mklev() in mklev.c and trace through makelevel(), etc...

